<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nile cataracts</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as fourth Nile cataract</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> cataracts are a series of shallow, rocky stretches of rapids on the Nile between Aswan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khartoum">Khartoum</a>, traditionally numbered from the first in the north to the sixth. They formed natural frontiers between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thutmose-i">Thutmose I</a>, the Egyptian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a> extended its reach as far as the fourth cataract.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nile-cataracts.jpg" alt="Nile cataracts" width="201" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Mark Dingemanse at English Wikipedia. · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nubia_today.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
